<h1>path</h1>
<p>
(definition)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A list of <a href="vertex.html"><em>vertices</em></a> of a <a href="graph.html"><em>graph</em></a> where each vertex has an <a href="edge.html"><em>edge</em></a> between it and the next vertex.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Specialization</strong> (... is a kind of me.)<br>
<a href="simplepath.html"><em>simple path</em></a>, <a href="shortestpath.html"><em>shortest path</em></a>, <a href="cycle.html"><em>cycle</em></a>, <a href="hamiltonianCycle.html"><em>Hamiltonian cycle</em></a>, <a href="eulercycle.html"><em>Euler cycle</em></a>, <a href="alterntngpth.html"><em>alternating path</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="allPairsShortestPath.html"><em>all pairs shortest path</em></a>, <a href="allSimplePaths.html"><em>all simple paths</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
A path is usually assumed to be a <a href="simplepath.html"><em>simple path</em></a>, unless otherwise defined. </p> 
<p> In a <a href="directedGraph.html"><em>directed graph</em></a>, each vertex has an edge from it to the next vertex.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>

